"A pentatonic scale has five notes.": "51f2c9226ef1",
"A pentatonic scale is a musical scale with five notes per octave. Starting on C, the major pentatonic scale is C, D, E, G, and A. Pentatonic scales were developed independently by many ancient civilizations, and they are still used in various musical styles today. The black keys on a piano keyboard make a pentatonic scale. In Taiwan’s dictionary, the five tones 宮, 商, 角, 徵, and 羽 are the five notes of the old Chinese scale. We tested our own notes by computer: no two of the notes C, D, E, G, and A are a half step apart.": "a8102908fd1a",
"A pentatonic scale is a musical scale with five notes per octave. Starting on C, the major pentatonic scale is C, D, E, G, and A. Pentatonic scales were developed independently by many ancient civilizations, and they are still used in various musical styles today. The black keys on a piano keyboard make a pentatonic scale. In Taiwan’s dictionary, the five tones 宮, 商, 角, 徵, and 羽 are the five notes of the old Chinese scale. We tested our own notes by computer: no two of the notes C, D, E, G, and A are a half step apart. Teng Yu-hsien was a Hakka composer, born on July 21, 1906, in the Longtan area of Taoyuan, in Japanese-ruled Taiwan. He died on June 11, 1944. At fifteen he entered the Taipei Normal School, a teacher-training school, and studied music there. He played the cello, the violin, the piano, and the mandolin. In 1925 he became a teacher at a public school. He later studied the theory of composition at a music college in Tokyo, and he returned to Taiwan in 1930. In 1933, when he was 28, a Columbia record company invited him to be its full-time composer, and in the same year he wrote songs such as “Longing for the Spring Breeze” and “Worry on a Moonlit Night”. Four of his best-known songs, “Rainy Night Flower”, “Longing for the Spring Breeze”, “Worry on a Moonlit Night”, and “Four Seasons Red”, are grouped together under the name 四月望雨. In 1940 he left the record industry and went back to teaching, at a school in Xinzhu. The Hakka Affairs Council says he left behind nearly 100 songs, many of which continue to be sung today. “Longing for the Spring Breeze” was voted the most popular 100-year-old song in Taiwan. Chinese Wikipedia describes the melody of this song as using the pentatonic gong mode. But who wrote the words? The words of “Longing for the Spring Breeze” were written by Li Lin-chiu, and the words of “Rainy Night Flower” by Chou Tien-wang. A song often has a composer and a lyricist, who are different people. “Rainy Night Flower” began as a children’s song called “Spring”. It was written in 1933 for Taiwanese children by the writer Liao Han-chen, and he gave it to Teng to set to music. Many of Teng’s songs were later banned, and some were rewritten into Japanese. A record of a Japanese popular song that was thought to be lost was rediscovered by a collector in 2007. An asteroid, 255989 Dengyushian, was named in his honor. Why do we not play his tunes or print his words here? Taiwan’s copyright law protects a work during the author’s life and 50 years after death, and the term ends on the last day of that year. By this rule, a composer who died in 1944 has been out of copyright since 1995, but the lyricists lived longer, so their words are still protected. Recordings and arrangements have their own terms. A composer’s moral rights, such as the right to be named and to keep a work from being distorted, continue even after death. So this lesson tells you the facts and the song names, and you write your own tune.": "25b53d372bb0",
